    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d454">

                <pb facs="0524" n="420" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg420"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4359: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan (Forbes) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-23T18:00:00+09:00">February 23, 1932—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 23—7:33 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>74. The British Military Attaché has just arrived from Shanghai with very
                    interesting details, indicating that the Japanese advanced about 5 kilometers in
                    the open region north of Shanghai but that very little advance was made in the
                    thickly settled part of the city and it is anticipated they will continue their
                    movements more in the open region outside. It is reported that the Chinese are
                    offering stiff opposition in the city.</p>
                <p>A report is just in that after a meeting of the Cabinet it was decided to send
                    the 11th Division to Shanghai. This was requested by their Minister and Consul
                    General and not by General Uyeda.</p>
                <p>I saw the French, German, British and Italian Ambassadors yesterday. From all
                    sources it is evident that the Japanese are very anxious to terminate the
                    Shanghai situation at the earliest possible moment. It seems that if the
                    fighting could be stopped the Japanese would not insist on a very extensive
                    withdrawal on the part of the Chinese. Discussing the Manchurian situation, it
                    was the opinion of my colleagues that independent Manchuria state was
                    inadmissible and could not be recognized by the powers; that there might be an
                    autonomous local government set up in Manchuria but the customs schedules and
                    general laws of China would be in force there and the territories of course
                    recognized as Chinese.</p>
                <p>Nagai tells Neville that in his opinion an independent Manchuria is
                    impracticable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Forbes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
